[0.009, "o", "\r\n==============================================================================\r\nQ: Did our signups fall from last month?\r\n==============================================================================\r\n\r\nConfidence: insufficient evidence\r\nTook 63s over 6 steps, 23,896 tokens, 17 observations gathered, 12 cited\r\n\r\nANSWER\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  No — signups did not fall. The daily signup rate held steady at roughly 157/day across both July and August (through August 12, the latest data available), with no decline visible in either the signup event stream or GA4 sessions.  [posthog.event_trend 7c37c2ab, ga4.get_sessions 0921fc98]\r\n\r\nFINDINGS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  Daily signup rate is flat across July and August  (high)\r\n    - Daily signup counts stayed in a narrow 149-165 range for every day from July 1 through August 12, with no downward trend visible at any point in that window.  [postho"]
[0.001, "o", "g.event_trend 7c37c2ab]\r\n    - GA4 session counts show the same flat pattern over the same period, ranging from about 149 to 165 sessions per day with no decline.  [ga4.get_sessions 0921fc98]\r\n    - A weekly rollup of signups shows 1,095 in the week of Aug 3 versus 315 in the partial week of Jul 27 and 459 in the partial week of Aug 10, but these buckets cover unequal numbers of days within each week, so they cannot be read as a decline without adjusting for that.  [posthog.event_trend 2179ad18]\r\n  A Slack message raised the concern, but no supporting data or decision record was found  (medium)\r\n    - On August 11 a team member in the #growth channel asked whether signups looked down that month versus July and whether a pricing redesign was hurting the numbers, but no decision record or further discussion on a pricing redesign was found in Slack.  [slack.search_messages 668ff4c4, slack.find_decision e95af626]\r\n\r\nCHARTS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  user sig"]
[0.000, "o", "ned up over time\r\n    value              |▁█▂|  range 315–1,095\r\n                        2026-07-27 … 2026-08-10\r\n\r\n  sessions over time\r\n    sessions           |▄▁▅▇█▆█▁▃█▆▄▇▅▆▃▂▄▂▄█▃▁▁▂▆▃▃▁█▄▂▁▁▂▆▂▁▅▃█▂▅|  range 149–165\r\n                        2026-07-01 … 2026-08-12\r\n\r\n  user signed up over time\r\n    value              |▄▁█▇▅▃█▅▇▇▅▄▅▄▂▃▇▁▁▅▃▅▄▃█▂▄▂▅▃▃▇▃▅█▂▁▃▇▆▃▃▂|  range 149–164\r\n                        2026-07-01 … 2026-08-12\r\n\r\n\r\nRISKS AND CAVEATS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - PostHog and GA4 data both end at August 12, so neither source can speak to the second half of August or any of September; the flat-rate conclusion is based only on the first 12 days of August and the answer to 'last month' (August in full, or September vs August) cannot be fully confirmed b"]
[0.000, "o", "eyond that date.\r\n  - HubSpot contacts, GitHub commit history for all four repos, feature flag changes, and PostHog annotations all returned empty for the relevant window, so a code or pricing change behind the Slack concern cannot be ruled in or out from this evidence.\r\n  - 1 claim(s) were removed because an independent check found the cited evidence did not support them. Treat the remaining conclusions with corresponding caution.\r\n  - 1 claim(s) go beyond their evidence and are retained with reduced confidence rather than removed, because the rest of each one holds. What the check found:\r\n- findings[1].claims[0]: asked as an open question, with this claim withheld, the same evidence does not establish a cause: The comparison returned no usable data: the current period has 0 days of data and\r\n  - 1 causal claim(s) were withheld: a separate check of the evidence gathered, made without seeing this report, found that it does not support a definitive answer about cause. All the numeric series stop hard on 2026-0"]
[0.000, "o", "8-12 with zero rows afterward, and every candidate-cause query (commits, HubSpot, feature flags, Slack decisions) came back empty \\u2014 that shows nobody found a cause, not that none exists, and it cannot distinguish a genuine signup decline from data simply not being recorded past that date. There's no dated change inside the window that plausibly explains any movement, so a cause cannot be established from this evidence. What would settle it: Session/signup data for the full comparison period beyond 2026-08-12, to confirm the data isn't simply missing due to a tracking/ingest gap; A dated deploy, config, or tracking-code change around the onset of the data cutoff (July 15\\u2013Aug 12) explaining why collection stopped; Confirmation from GA4/PostHog ingestion status logs w\r\n\r\nDATA QUALITY\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - Both GA4 sessions and PostHog signup events are only populated through 2026-08-12; no data exists for the remainder of August or for Sept"]
[0.000, "o", "ember in the evidence gathered.\r\n  - 8 of the 12 cited observation(s) returned no results (ga4.compare_periods, github.commits, hubspot.contacts, posthog.annotations, posthog.feature_flags, slack.find_decision). Conclusions resting on them describe what was not found, which is not the same as what does not exist.\r\n\r\nSOURCES\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  2c57f566  ga4.compare_periods  eval://ga4__compare_periods\r\n  0921fc98  ga4.get_sessions  eval://ga4__get_sessions\r\n  da03cb1d  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  e7c8db47  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  af90789f  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  1440a09b  hubspot.contacts  eval://hubspot__contacts\r\n  75263a29  posthog.annotations  eval://posthog__annotations\r\n  2179ad18  posthog.event_trend  eval://posthog__event_trend\r\n  7c37c2ab  posthog.event_trend  eval://posthog__event_trend\r\n  ead82a92  posthog.feature_flags  eval://posthog__feature_flags\r\n  e95af626  slack.find_decision  ev"]
